{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>I will behave wisely, wholehearted</b> — a description of the way, like: 'and make my way wholehearted' [2 Samuel 22:33]." "<b>When will you come to me</b> — the meaning: for he will always act wisely at every moment, to accustom himself to walk in it, until he feels no effort in walking, and it is as though the way comes to him of itself, like the way of a harp through much practice. And Rabbi Moses [ibn Ezra] said: 'When will you come to me' is like: 'and Elohim came to Balaam' [Numbers 23:4] — that the holy spirit would rest upon him in his house when he secluded himself from people and their laws and their strife. And whichever interpretation is the correct one, thus he explained to me, and whichever interpretation is the correct one speaks of the time of his seclusion and separation. And behold, the meaning of 'I will walk' is habit, like: 'and Enoch walked' [Genesis 5:22]."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 101, :notes {1 "The Hebrew appears to preserve dittographic or dictation artifacts (the clause 'ואיזה פירוש שיהיה הוא הנכון' repeats with variation). Transmitted hyper-literally without smoothing. 'Rabbi Moses' resolved as Moses ibn Ezra by inference."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:37:44.809983687Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}